00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:04.606
- When you look back at that Super Bowl season, 

00:01:04.606 --> 00:01:06.524
take me through that year.
What do you remember from that year?

00:01:06.524 --> 00:01:08.151
- That started with getting traded.

00:01:08.151 --> 00:01:11.571
I was at Disney [Land] when I got the call to say I was going to be traded.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:14.908
So then my daughter's birthday was around, and we were going to Disney [Land].

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:16.701
Bill Belichick called me and 

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:19.162
he's like, "Hey, we need to get you out here for your physical" or whatever.

00:01:19.162 --> 00:01:22.290
I'm like, "Well, coach, I'm at Disney [Land] right now. I literally cannot come."

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.416
- [Laughing]

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:25.251
- And I'm trying to enjoy Disney [Land] with my family. 

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:27.921
Mickey is there, Goofy is there, everyone is there, you know?

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:30.381
It's just like, "These guys are great." And at the same time I've been traded.

00:01:30.381 --> 00:01:31.716
Bill called me back. 

00:01:31.716 --> 00:01:33.218
He's like, "Well, we got to get you out here;

00:01:33.218 --> 00:01:35.595
otherwise the trade won't go through if we don't get the physical."

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:37.514
And I was just kind of like, "Well,

00:01:37.514 --> 00:01:39.140
that sounds like a you thing."

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.600
- [Laughing]

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.478
- But then my agent called me, he was like, "Hey, no. You got to go. 

00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:44.395
You got to go." 

00:01:44.395 --> 00:01:49.067
And I was like, "I can't go tomorrow, like two days from now? 
Why I got to stop my life right now 

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:50.985
to fly to fucking Boston 

00:01:50.985 --> 00:01:54.239
to go see this motherfucker and go do a MRI for them?

00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:57.700
I'm here right now trying to—I'm at Disney [Land] for crying out loud. Not like I’m—"

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.910
- For your daughter's birthday.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:00.453
- Yeah, they don't give a fuck. 

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:01.996
But then Tom calls me.

00:02:01.996 --> 00:02:02.664
- Tom Brady?

00:02:02.664 --> 00:02:03.790
- Tom Brady, yeah, Tom.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.293
- We all don't have a personal one-on-one connection with Tom Brady 
to just call him Tom.

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:10.922
- Well me and Tom became cool because of our love for interior design.

00:02:10.922 --> 00:02:11.381
- What?

00:02:11.381 --> 00:02:13.633
- We bonded over Pinterest.

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:16.344
- Wait. You and Tom Brady would just surf Pinterest?

00:02:16.344 --> 00:02:17.512
- Yeah. So Tom calls me,

00:02:17.512 --> 00:02:19.764
and he was like, "Man, I'm so excited about getting you out here."

00:02:19.764 --> 00:02:22.016
I was just kind of like, "Yes, but Tom, 

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:25.436
do I have to go out there right now cause I feel like they're kind of 
making this shit up right now? 

00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:27.856
I don't know how this goes. Like do I have to come?"

00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:29.190
He's like, "Yeah, bud, 

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.651
you kinda have to go do that right now." I was like, "All right.”

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:38.283
- So how did that season go? For someone like you as you're talking about your career,

00:02:38.283 --> 00:02:40.910
I mean that season for a lot of people was a season 

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:42.495
many people won't forget.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:45.081
Even the casual fan remembers that Patriots' season.

00:02:45.081 --> 00:02:47.458
- For me, I think that was the most fun I ever had playing football 

00:02:47.458 --> 00:02:52.881
from Pee Wee to just any time on any team. It was just fun. Like all the guys were cool.

00:02:52.881 --> 00:02:57.093
No one bothered me about, like, what I was doing or if I was 
at art museums or whatever. 

00:02:57.093 --> 00:03:01.347
And I respected the way that they treated people like men.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:02.724
-Mmm, what that mean?
- Right?

00:03:02.724 --> 00:03:06.019
A lot of these coaches talk to players as if they're boys, right?

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:09.647
I'm like, "I got a wife and kid, coach. What the fuck are you 
talking about, man? I mean, shit.

00:03:10.315 --> 00:03:12.400
- Did you ever grow up wanting to be a Super Bowl champion?

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.945
- That's a tough question because I don't think it was my idea of it.

00:03:15.945 --> 00:03:19.449
First person that I experienced it with was when my brother won a Super Bowl.

00:03:19.449 --> 00:03:21.743
And he told me it was like having another child.

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:24.287
So when my brother won, we were talking about it. 

00:03:24.287 --> 00:03:26.122
And first of all, he's an asshole.

00:03:26.122 --> 00:03:27.790
Because I was in Seattle and like, 

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:28.791
I was staying at his house. 

00:03:28.791 --> 00:03:32.795
I was like, "Hey, man, you got some of that orange juice?" 
He's like, "Yeah. Go get some out the refrigerator." 

00:03:32.795 --> 00:03:38.259
I open it and there's a Super Bowl trophy, you know what I’m saying? He just had
this thing like you get in the car and its in the car with him, you know what I’m saying?

00:03:38.259 --> 00:03:41.971
He was like, "Yo this is the greatest feeling ever, etc., etc., etc.” 

00:03:41.971 --> 00:03:44.974
And so for me, I'm like, "Damn, that sounds good. I want to feel that.

00:03:44.974 --> 00:03:48.144
- But it was different in New England. Going in the Super Bowl,
what was that experience like?

00:03:48.144 --> 00:03:49.312
- Yeah, we were getting our ass kicked.

00:03:49.312 --> 00:03:52.440
I mean, what the fuck were the Falcons doing? 
They were celebrating at the halftime.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:53.441
- [Laughing]

00:03:53.441 --> 00:03:56.861
- One thing I remember, I think it was 2 Chainz who came down like at the halftime.

00:03:56.861 --> 00:04:00.323
That I remember their sideline more so than our sideline.

00:04:00.615 --> 00:04:02.116
2 Chainz, T.I.,

00:04:02.116 --> 00:04:05.995
it was like everybody you will imagine being 
at the strip club without the strippers.

00:04:05.995 --> 00:04:08.248
Man, Tupac was probably on that sideline.

00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:09.916
- [Laughing]

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:11.918
- Cuz looking at their sideline, you'd be like,

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:14.462
"These motherfuckers really think they won already!"

00:04:14.462 --> 00:04:15.713
- Take me through the second half. 

00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:17.423
During that come back as it happens, 

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:20.426
what's going on in your mind as you see the Falcons slipping this thing away?

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:21.219
- That's the whole thing:

00:04:21.219 --> 00:04:21.970
nothing.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.137
I was like water, 

00:04:23.137 --> 00:04:24.806
and all my teammates were like water. 

00:04:24.806 --> 00:04:30.353
Basically it was just like so focused, and enter a space of flow 
that I've never experienced in sports before.

00:04:30.353 --> 00:04:32.188
In fact, when we won, 

00:04:32.188 --> 00:04:35.400
I had to ask Tom Brady. I said, "Yo, Tom, did we win?" 

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.318
He was like, "Yeah. We're champions." 

00:04:37.318 --> 00:04:38.736
I said, "Oh, shit...

00:04:38.736 --> 00:04:39.404
- You didn’t know you won?

00:04:39.404 --> 00:04:43.199
- We champions!" I don't know the fucking overtime rules in Super Bowl. 
Shit, they changed it.

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:43.950
- [Laughing]

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.245
I'm telling you, bro. I was in flow. Like, I don't know what the score is, right?

00:04:47.245 --> 00:04:52.000
I had no idea. Like, I just, that was my only time of like 
really snapping out of flow, 

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.336
cause I'm looking around and guys are running around. We're all crying, 

00:04:55.336 --> 00:04:56.963
and I don't know why I'm crying. 

00:04:56.963 --> 00:05:00.133
My brother comes down, and I think I was extra emotional. 

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:03.761
"We're Super Bowl champions, the Bennett brothers. Like who the fuck can say that shit?”

00:05:04.679 --> 00:05:08.641
- I thought you were going to be really damn good at this game.  
How many episodes of this have you done?

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:09.726
- [Laughing]

00:05:09.726 --> 00:05:11.519
- What's going on in your mind in Chicago 

00:05:11.519 --> 00:05:13.354
as now you finally left Boston? 

00:05:13.354 --> 00:05:16.316
You've done everything that you had to do that a Super Bowl champion does 

00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:18.568
- It may be the pinnacle for somebody, but like 

00:05:18.568 --> 00:05:22.447
it was never that pinnacle moment for my life. I actually felt like, "Damn, 

00:05:22.447 --> 00:05:24.866
I've dedicated 20 years of my life for this shit?" 

00:05:24.866 --> 00:05:26.951
I didn't make the world a better place, right?

00:05:26.951 --> 00:05:32.498
I didn't make any contributions to changing the world and 
the idea of what I feel like should change the world.

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:36.461
- So now off season, you're sitting in this space where you're like, "I spent 
20 years doing this; it wasn't worth it." 

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:37.837
What are you doing that offseason then?

00:05:37.837 --> 00:05:43.885
- I had to get another job because I was just in between jobs. I ended up 
going to the Packers, which was fucking disastrous from day one.

00:05:43.885 --> 00:05:46.095
After that, I ended back with the Patriots. 

00:05:46.095 --> 00:05:52.477
I went on IR, but when I went on IR I had an injury that I couldn't really fix, 
which was a broken spirit.

00:05:52.477 --> 00:05:58.441
And there's no type of treatment that the NFL could provide for a broken spirit. 
There's nothing they could do for me at this point.

00:05:58.441 --> 00:06:02.820
There’s nothing Bill Belichick could say, Tom or the trainers. 
My spirit was broken in a way that 

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.739
there was something else I was looking for that 

00:06:04.739 --> 00:06:06.616
no one in the sporting world could give me.

00:06:06.616 --> 00:06:09.869
The only thing that could truly heal was art and just making things. 

00:06:09.869 --> 00:06:12.580
When I make things is when I feel most like myself.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:16.876
So after that, you know, I kind of rekindled this love I had with Japanese culture.

00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:22.256
You know I have been studying, you know, Wabi-Sabi and yokai and 
Shintoism and all these things. 

00:06:22.256 --> 00:06:25.218
I told my wife, I was like, "Man, I really feel like I should go to Japan."

00:06:25.218 --> 00:06:26.094
- How long were you there for?

00:06:26.094 --> 00:06:27.512
- I was there for 10 days. 

00:06:27.512 --> 00:06:29.472
Tom Brady had a guy over there named Leonard

00:06:29.472 --> 00:06:31.057
they put me in connection with.

00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:35.019
Basically all I did was I studied the Japanese culture, I prayed to the Japanese gods, 

00:06:35.019 --> 00:06:40.650
learned language, learned their art. I had my notebook, taking notes, 
drawing things and like just taking it all in. Like I was very present. 

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:45.655
And it made me reflect like, "Damn, why the fuck do I do something 
that I don't love every single part of?" 

00:06:45.655 --> 00:06:50.118
After that, I came back. I retired like a week after coming back from Japan. 
I told my wife, I was like, "I'm retiring."

00:06:50.118 --> 00:06:52.745
So I did a little art piece and I did that, then I retired. 

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:56.040
But it was really because of the connection in Japan with the people. 

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.542
- Do you still feel like you have a broken spirit?

00:06:57.542 --> 00:07:00.837
- Do I have a broken spirit? Hell nah. I'm fucking rich as fuck.

00:07:00.837 --> 00:07:03.131
Meaning, rich as of mind and spirit. 

00:07:03.131 --> 00:07:05.591
I'm the healthiest, I'm a better husband.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:10.012
I didn't want my daughter to grow up with a football dad because 
that enigma could take over what your dad is. 

00:07:10.012 --> 00:07:13.266
If my daughter be like, "My dad traveled the world. He spent summers in Japan.

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:18.479
You know, he built video games. He changed the world. 
He brought Internet infrastructure to underserved communities."

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:19.814
That's my legacy. 

00:07:19.814 --> 00:07:22.567
Right? Football is not my legacy, and it never was. 

00:07:22.567 --> 00:07:24.777
I feel like I'm a better me without the game of football.

